<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Architecture', href: '/docs/architecture' }, { label: 'Agents', href: '/docs/architecture/agents' }, { label: 'FORGE (Builder)' }]}>
  <h1>FORGE  -  Builder Agent</h1>

  <p>
    FORGE is the <strong>builder</strong>. It writes code against an agreed plan, creates branches, and opens pull
    requests. It never builds without an approved plan — it plans, then halts, and only implements once its plan
    has been reviewed and approved.
  </p>

  <p>
    FORGE runs inside its own isolated, disposable workspace, provisioned for exactly one ticket and torn down when the
    work merges. It coordinates with the team through the standard command surface, and it holds <strong>no LLM API
    keys, no raw repository credentials</strong>, and has tightly restricted network access. Git identity comes from a
    scoped OAuth link, and all AI calls are routed through the central gateway in the control plane.
  </p>

  <h2>What FORGE does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Plans, then halts.</strong> FORGE writes a plan — a breakdown of the work — before writing any code. It stops there and waits for approval. This is the "plan before code" guardrail.</li>
    <li><strong>Implements the approved plan.</strong> Only after the plan is approved does FORGE write code, one step at a time.</li>
    <li><strong>Runs tests.</strong> FORGE verifies its own work as it goes.</li>
    <li><strong>Opens a pull request.</strong> Once the work is reviewed and approved, FORGE opens the pull request for merging.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it fits into the team</h2>
  <p>
    NEXUS assigns a ticket to an idle FORGE worker and provisions its workspace. FORGE plans and waits; SENTINEL
    reviews; FORGE implements against the approved plan; SENTINEL reviews the result; and once approved, FORGE opens
    the pull request that VESSEL will merge.
  </p>

  <Callout type="info" title="Bounded by gates, not by permission prompts">
    FORGE acts directly — it doesn't need to ask permission for every action — but its actions are
    bounded by gates. It can't build without an approved plan, and it can't open a pull request before its
    work has been reviewed and approved. This keeps a direct builder from running ahead of the plan.
  </Callout>
</DocsLayout>
